    "raw": "{\"ruleId\":\"item-097dec07ae01\",\"class\":\"partial\",\"constraints\":[{\"id\":\"legacy-msg-segwit-ts-js\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"pattern\":\"\\\\bsegwitType\\\\s*:\\\\s*['\\\"]p2(wpkh|sh\\\\(p2wpkh\\\\))['\\\"]\",\"path\":\".floor/added/**/*.?s\"},{\"id\":\"legacy-msg-segwit-tsx-jsx\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"pattern\":\"\\\\bsegwitType\\\\s*:\\\\s*['\\\"]p2(wpkh|sh\\\\(p2wpkh\\\\))['\\\"]\",\"path\":\".floor/added/**/*.?sx\"},{\"id\":\"legacy-msg-segwit-mjs\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"pattern\":\"\\\\bsegwitType\\\\s*:\\\\s*['\\\"]p2(wpkh|sh\\\\(p2wpkh\\\\))['\\\"]\",\"path\":\".floor/added/**/*.mjs\"}],\"coverage\":\"Added lines in .ts/.js/.tsx/.jsx/.mjs files that pass a bitcoinjs-message-style `segwitType: 'p2wpkh'` or `'p2sh(p2wpkh)'` option on one line. That option asks for a legacy (BIP-137) signed-message signature for a segwit address, which is a flagrant literal violation.\",\"residual\":\"Does the hunk sign or verify messages with the legacy `Bitcoin Signed Message:` format for a segwit or taproot address, or use that format in a newly written signing path, in a way not caught by a single-line `segwitType: 'p2wpkh'` or `segwitType: 'p2sh(p2wpkh)'` option in an added JS/TS line?\",\"probes\":{\"violating\":[{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/lib/sign.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1,2 @@\\n+import * as bitcoinMessage from \\\"bitcoinjs-message\\\";\\n+export const sig = (m: string, k: Buffer) => bitcoinMessage.sign(m, k, true, { segwitType: \\\"p2wpkh\\\" });\\n\"},{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/lib/Sign.tsx\\n@@ -0,0 +1,2 @@\\n+import * as bitcoinMessage from \\\"bitcoinjs-message\\\";\\n+export const sig = (m: string, k: Buffer) => bitcoinMessage.sign(m, k, true, { segwitType: 'p2sh(p2wpkh)' });\\n\"},{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/lib/sign.mjs\\n@@ -0,0 +1,2 @@\\n+import bitcoinMessage from \\\"bitcoinjs-message\\\";\\n+export const sig = (m, k) => bitcoinMessage.sign(m, k, true, { segwitType: \\\"p2wpkh\\\" });\\n\"}],\"compliant\":{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/lib/sign.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1,2 @@\\n+import { Signer } from \\\"bip322-js\\\";\\n+export const sig = (a: string, k: string, m: string) => Signer.sign(k, a, m);\\n\"}},\"rationale\":\"A `segwitType` p2wpkh option in a legacy message-signing call is a literal, mechanical sign of BIP-137 signing for segwit, so it can be checked by pattern. Other cases need judgement: knowing the address type, spotting taproot use, telling a new signing path apart from allowed P2PKH verification, and other libraries or languages. These depend on what the code means and how data flows, which line patterns cannot decide.\"}",
